  "headline": "Oil is squeezing growth: 5% yields, a peak dollar and no shelter in gold",
  "body": [
    "The read is stagflationary, with the dollar as the pressure valve. Brent at $104 is up 36% over three months and 70% year to date; the 10-year, at 5.23%, has climbed 43 basis points this month to the top of its 52-week range; the Dollar Index is up 3.4% on the month at a one-year high, with USD/CAD and USD/INR pinned at range highs. Gold, down 5% on the month and near its range low, says this is a real-rates story, not a fear trade: haven demand is going into dollars, not bullion.",
    "The editor's note points to the Saudi-Yemeni escalation behind today's oil spike. The numbers bear it out as an inflation shock rather than a panic: Brent closed up 2% with volatility 1.3 times its own-year norm, copper fell 2.9% on the day and bitcoin is off 3.3% on the week, while yields eased only 5 basis points and gold barely moved. It is an increment on an oil advance already well under way.",
    "The tensions: copper at 84% of its range and a copper/gold ratio up 2% on the month read as growth, and bitcoin buying 10% more gold than a month ago reads as liquidity, but both are mostly gold's weakness. The bear-flattening curve, with the 30-10 spread down 8 basis points to 38, carries more weight: that is the signature of inflation and policy repricing, not a growth boom."
  ],
